  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">**Empirically determine the discriminator.** Decode a real L2 UAMI token (or the probe's token-acquisition path + Entra docs for the `DefaultAzureCredential` client-credentials flow against a BFF `.default` scope) and establish which claims are actually present: `idtyp`, `roles`, `scp`, `oid`, `sub`, `appid`, `azp`. Choose the POSITIVE app-only signal from what is really there. Record the evidence in notes — the entire design hangs on this answer, and guessing it is how this task ships a bypass.</step>
    <step order="1">Reproduce the defect: authenticate as tenant A, request `?tenantId={B}`, observe resolution. This is the test that must go red-then-green.</step>
    <step order="2">Write the primitive in `Spaarke.Core/Auth/`. Three outcomes minimum — user-delegated / application (carrying the app id) / indeterminate. Pure function over `ClaimsPrincipal`, no ambient state, no `HttpContext`.</step>
    <step order="3">Repoint `AuditEnrichmentMiddleware.IsOnBehalfOfFlow` at the primitive. Its two-valued logging answer is a projection of the three-valued classification — preserve its current behaviour for logging (indeterminate logs as not-OBO) while the authorization path treats indeterminate as deny. Note that asymmetry in a comment so it does not read as an inconsistency.</step>
    <step order="4">Gate the endpoint: application ∧ allow-listed → may name any `tenantId`; everything else → 403 BEFORE any resolver call. Deny with a uniform response so a denied caller learns nothing about which tenants exist. Config key + its absence-denies semantics documented at the read site.</step>
    <step order="5">Correct the two stale doc claims on the endpoint (class summary "parity with all other BFF endpoints" assurance; `WithDescription`'s "falling back to the caller's JWT tid claim").</step>
    <step order="6">Tests — see `acceptance-criteria` for the closed set. Perturbation-verify: neutralize the app-only determination (accept `appid` alone) and confirm the delegated-token test goes RED. If it does not, the trap is still live.</step>
    <step order="7">Verify the L2 probe path still resolves: an app-only allow-listed token naming a foreign tenant gets 200 with the tenant echoed. Build, full test run, publish size, CVE scan.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If Step 0 finds NO claim that positively distinguishes app-only from user-delegated in the tokens this BFF actually receives, STOP. Do not fall back to inferring app-only from absence, and do not fall back to `appid`-only. That would ship the trap. Escalate — the answer is then an app role / required scope on the L2 app registration, which needs the other project's agreement.</trigger>
    <trigger>If placing the primitive in `Spaarke.Core` requires an AspNetCore FrameworkReference or a new package, STOP and escalate. Do not relocate it BFF-side — that silently forfeits the reason this task is worth doing now.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the fix cannot be made without editing the L2 probe, STOP. The design has drifted back toward option C.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`AuditEnrichmentMiddleware.IsOnBehalfOfFlow` (:129-145) already classifies caller kind from `idtyp`/`scp`/`oid`, and `ResolveAppId` (:102-104) already reads `appid`/`azp` — both `private static` inside a logging middleware, unreachable by any authorization path. `TenantAuthorizationFilter` already enforces tenant-claim matching (option A) but cannot express "operators may name a tenant."</existing>
    <extension>PROMOTE the middleware's existing claim logic into `Spaarke.Core.Auth` and have the middleware consume it. Net new *logic* is the three-valued result and the allow-list policy; the claim reading is moved, not written twice. The alternative — a second claim reader in the BFF — is what §11 exists to prevent.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Any authenticated user of any tenant can enumerate which tenants this stamp serves and resolve their SPE container ids. On a multi-tenant platform that is customer-list disclosure. Live on master today.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
