    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Endpoints/Diagnostics/TenantContainerResolverEndpoint.cs — gate + doc corrections</output>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/Logging/AuditEnrichmentMiddleware.cs — delegates to the primitive</output>
    <output type="test">tests/unit/Spaarke.Core.Tests/ — classifier unit tests incl. the delegated-token-carrying-an-allow-listed-appid case</output>
    <output type="test">tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/ — endpoint gate tests</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-081-*.md — Step 0's claim evidence, and why option B does not need to touch the probe</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A user-delegated caller is DENIED — including when its `appid` IS in the allow-list. This is the trap test; it must fail without the positive app-only determination.</criterion>
    <criterion>An app-only caller whose `appid` is NOT in the allow-list is denied.</criterion>
    <criterion>An app-only caller whose `appid` IS in the allow-list resolves a foreign tenant successfully, with the tenant echoed — i.e. the L2 H13 I4 probe contract is intact.</criterion>
    <criterion>An empty allow-list denies everyone, including an otherwise-valid app-only caller.</criterion>
    <criterion>An absent/unconfigured allow-list denies everyone (distinct from the empty case — a fresh environment must not fail open).</criterion>
    <criterion>A caller with no resolvable tenant claim and no query parameter is denied, not defaulted to an ambient tenant.</criterion>
    <criterion>An indeterminate classification is denied.</criterion>
    <criterion>**The enumeration oracle is closed for unauthorized callers**: served and not-served tenants are indistinguishable to a DENIED caller, because denial happens before any resolver call. Note this is scoped to denied callers — a permitted operator MUST still see 400-vs-200, since distinguishing served from not-served is the probe's entire purpose. (The original version of this criterion was unscoped and would have broken the probe.)</criterion>
    <criterion>Exactly ONE classifier exists: grep confirms no `FindFirst("idtyp")` / `FindFirst("appid")` / `FindFirst("azp")` outside the primitive, except pre-existing outbound-token-inspection sites (`GraphClientFactory.cs:238`, `AuthorizationModule.cs:160`, `MiddlewarePipelineExtensions.cs:114-115` — these read tokens the BFF SENDS or logs, not inbound caller kind; leave them alone and say so).</criterion>
    <criterion>`Spaarke.Core.csproj` gains no new `PackageReference` and no `FrameworkReference`.</criterion>
    <criterion>The primitive is consumable from `Spaarke.Core/Auth/AuthorizationService.cs` — demonstrated by it compiling in that assembly, not by adding a speculative call site.</criterion>
    <criterion>ArchTests: `LayerDependencyTests` green, and task 074's census stays green (this task changes no route count).</criterion>
    <criterion>Both stale doc claims on the endpoint corrected.</criterion>
    <criterion>Build clean; `dotnet test` on the two touched unit projects + `Spaarke.ArchTests`; publish size ≤60 MB reported with the PDB convention stated; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Found by the census ratchet, not by a route inventory — the file post-dates all four prior enumerations of this surface. Third hole task 074's forcing function produced (after 077 and 078), and the first that came from being *made to classify* a new file rather than from a rule firing directly.

The owner's framing when choosing B: *"whether we fix in 081 or add to UAC evaluator either way we need this to be included — if 081 is a quick win but then ends up having to be replaced or redone as part of the evaluator then don't waste time."* The placement constraint is the entire answer to that. Get it wrong and this IS wasted time.</notes>
</task>
